  <activityDataset>
    <activityDescription>
      <activity id="e2de16d9-8da7-50d7-b852-48c22cf7db54" activityNameId="afa5f1e8-2a6e-500b-bf06-4c59c41fdf60" activityNameContextId="f44c43c1-5cc5-54a6-ade6-24435bd12c83" type="1" specialActivityType="0" energyValues="0">
        <activityName xml:lang="en">xx Disposal, residue from TiO2 prod. Cl, 56% water, to residual material landfill</activityName>
        <generalComment>
          <text xml:lang="en" index="1">Full activity name: xx Disposal, residue from TiO2 prod. Cl, 56% water, to residual material landfill
Inventoried waste contains 100% residue from TiO2 production (chloride process); . waste composition (wet, in ppm): H2O 560000; O 200000; H n.a.; C 90000; S n.a.; N n.a.; P 893.79; B 7.1933; Cl n.a.; Br n.a.; F n.a.; I n.a.; Ag 0.714; As 0.62521; Ba 149.04; Cd 11.748; Co 1.3453; Cr n.a.; Cu n.a.; Hg n.a.; Mn 259.36; Mo 1.9551; Ni n.a.; Pb 502.43; Sb 22.564; Se 0.31969; Sn 73.44; V 200; Zn 770; Be n.a.; Sc n.a.; Sr n.a.; Ti 80000; Tl n.a.; W n.a.; Si 20000; Fe 25000; Ca 22000; Al n.a.; K 2059.7; Mg n.a.; Na n.a.; Share of carbon in waste that is biogenic 0%. ;; UUID: 188872ed-2560-4d85-bd58-7cf5ee588519
Waste-specific short-term emissions to water from leachate. Long-term emissions from landfill to ground water. 
Location: Technology encountered in Switzerland in 2000. Landfill includes base seal and leachate collection system.
Technology: Swiss residual material landfill for polluted, inorganic waste. With base seal and leachate collection system. Recultivation after closure. 
Time period: Waste composition as given in literature reference, theoretical data or other source. Transfer coefficients from prospective model.
Production volume: na
Sampling: Landfill model based on observed leachate concentrations in literature. Extrapolated to 60'000 years heeding chemical characteristics. Initial waste composition from various literature sources. 
Extrapolations: &lt;null&gt;
Uncertainty adjustments: uncertainty of waste input composition data derived from generic formula GSD(c) = N*ln(c)+1. Minimal long-term emissions are the emissions until the carbonate buffer in the landfill is used up. Mean long-term emissions are the emissions until the next glacial period occurs (in 60'000a) and the landfill is eroded. Maximal long-term emissions are the complete emissions of all landfilled material (except Cr).
BAFU mapped export: unsupported biosphere exchanges excluded; see audit/. Local UUIDs and generic activity classification are export conventions. Source: process_188872ed-2560-4d85-bd58-7cf5ee588519.xml</text>
        </generalComment>
      </activity>
